  "user_scenario": "You are Desmond Kirkland, 41, a warehouse operations supervisor in Milwaukee who likes things organized and on autopilot. You are calling Larkspur Lending on Monday morning, calm but a little annoyed with yourself, about your PracticePay plan from Cedar Ridge Veterinary Hospital — the one you opened in April when your dog needed surgery. The six-month promotion on it ended last week and you did not manage to pay it off in full in time. Now you just want the plan set up properly going forward: you want to put it back on AutoPay at whatever the monthly payment is from here on, so you never have to think about it again. You will set the AutoPay up yourself if they give you the way to do it. You are not asking to pay anything off today and you are not asking about any other account.\n\nYou start by explaining that the promotion on your Cedar Ridge Veterinary Hospital plan has ended, that you couldn't clear it in time, and that you want to get it back on AutoPay at the payment it needs now. You give your mobile number, 4145553928, so they can pull up your account. It is the only PracticePay plan you have.\n\nWhen they ask you to verify, you give your date of birth, 1984-11-07, and then the last four of your Social Security number, 3516. If they need more, you can give your email, desmond.kirkland@outlook.com, or your ZIP code, 53202. You answer one question at a time.\n\nIf the agent asks why AutoPay is off, you say you switched it off at the start of the month because you were planning to pay the whole thing off with one big payment, but you only managed part of it. If they ask which bank account the AutoPay should come from, you say the Landmark Credit Union checking account ending in 3390, the one already on file. Only if the agent brings up the billing dispute do you say the clinic had billed the overnight stay twice back in May and refunded it in June, and that is all you remember about it. You do not know what the new payment should be and have no number in mind; you are counting on the agent to tell you. You do not raise anything else about the account on your own.\n\nIf the agent tells you that some things have to be put through on the plan on their end before the new payment can be set, you say that is fine and they can go ahead. When the agent tells you the new monthly payment and gives you the way to set it up, you confirm it yourself for your plan at exactly the amount they told you, with AutoPay turned on, and read back what the confirmation shows. If the agent mentions that the payment due on the 20th was already billed at the old amount, you say that is fine.\n\nOnce the agent confirms the new payment and AutoPay are in place, you thank them, say that is all you needed, and hang up. You do not ask for anything else.",
